{
  "heading": "Error handlers in every Yon language",
  "axis": "language",
  "body": "An error handler turns a typed error into a public answer, in one place for all routes (ADR 0051). Choose a language below. GET /orders throws OrderNotFound. The handler answers 404 with {\"code\":\"order-not-found\"}. The error type lives in server/errors/ and has no annotation. The handler class lives in server/controllers/ and its name ends with ErrorHandler. HandlesError takes no argument: the type of the first parameter says which error the method handles. Ruby names that type in a YARD tag, # @param error [OrderNotFound]. An optional second parameter receives the request in the language's own type; a PHP handler reads the superglobals instead. ResponseStatus sets a status from 400 to 599. Tachyon checks the answer against the Problem model, and OPTIONS and /api.json list OrderNotFound with status 404 and the Problem shape. The nearest type wins: a handler for a subclass runs before a handler for its base class. Go matches with errors.As, so a wrapped error matches too. Rust matches the error type of the controller method's Result. An error that no method handles, or an error thrown inside a handler, keeps the fixed 500 answer. Nothing from the error reaches the client unless the handler returns it. The order is: controller, then error handler, then middleware after.\n\nC++ has no tab here, because C++ Yon code has only route controllers so far: the build refuses `ErrorHandler` in C++ with `TY2015` (ADR 0099).",
